rime(x)=\\frac{5}{3 x}.$ is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3 x}\\cdot 3 \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv \\frac{5}{3 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true. Substituting $y=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}3 e^{- y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3}{e^{\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} &=\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is true. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true, the function $y=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement”; “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” only." 13194139537080 17592186576411 true] [17592186577195 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3 x}\\cdot 3 \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv \\frac{5}{3 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}3 e^{- y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3}{e^{\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} &=\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537080 17592186576411 false] [17592186577201 1077 "$f\\prime(x)=\\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}.$" 13194139537080 17592186576411 true] [17592186577201 1077 "I" 13194139537080 17592186576411 false] [17592186577202 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=2 e^{- y},\\qquad 2 x - 3>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537080 17592186576411 true] [17592186577202 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=2 e^{- y},\\qquad 2 x - 3>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{2}{2 x - 3}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." 13194139537080 17592186576411 false] [17592186577202 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{2}{2 x - 3}.$ is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 x - 3}\\cdot 2 \\\\ &=\\frac{2}{2 x - 3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” i